          "title": "You'll want to know: maereō, maerēre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "maereō, maerēre — \"to grieve, mourn\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Senex maeret, quod amīcus eius periit. The old man grieves because his friend has died."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "maereō, maerēre, maeruī; no supine in common use. Sadness that shows, as a mourner's does."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "maereō is a native Latin verb; Lewis & Short compares Greek misos \"hatred\" and Latin miser \"wretched\", but EtymDB gives no parent, so the origin is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
